          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled JSON numbers reach unguarded BigDecimal conversions in JsonNumberImpl, bypassing the bigIntegerScaleLimit defense. The public accessors intValue() (line 262, sink 264), intValueExact() (267, sink 269), longValue() (272, sink 274), longValueExact() (277, sink 279) and doubleValue() (282, sink 284) all call bigDecimalValue().<conv>() with NO scale check, while the immediately following siblings bigIntegerValue() (287) and bigIntegerValueExact() (297) DO guard with `Math.abs(bd.scale()) <= bigIntegerScaleLimit` and otherwise throw UnsupportedOperationException(NUMBER_SCALE_LIMIT_EXCEPTION). The other number defense does not cover this: JsonTokenizer.getBigDecimal only limits the *source character count* (`sourceLen > jsonContext.bigDecimalLengthLimit()`, line 522, DEFAULT_MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN=1100), so a 13-character JSON literal such as {\"a\":1E+2147483647} passes the length check and yields a BigDecimal with scale -2147483647, which then flows unguarded into intValueExact()/longValueExact(). Source: JsonValueBodyReader.readFrom (rest/.../JsonValueBodyReader.java:69-70, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1) parses an untrusted HTTP entity with Json.createReaderFactory(null) (defaults), and JsonParserImpl:171 builds the number via getJsonNumber(getBigDecimal(), jsonContext.bigIntegerScaleLimit()). This is the same incomplete-fix shape as the original scale-limit hardening: the limit was applied to the BigInteger accessors only. A verifier must confirm the JDK BigDecimal behaviour (intValueExact/longValueExact perform setScale(0)/toBigInteger, materializing a ~2^31-digit unscaled value) to establish the CPU/memory exhaustion.\n\nVerifier (INCONCLUSIVE): The structural claim is real and I reproduced it: JsonNumberImpl.intValue/intValueExact/longValue/longValueExact/doubleValue (lines 262-285) convert via bigDecimalValue() with no scale check, while the immediately following bigIntegerValue/bigIntegerValueExact (287-305) do enforce Math.abs(bd.scale()) <= bigIntegerScaleLimit; and the other number defense really is only a source-character limit (JsonTokenizer.java:522, DEFAULT_MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN=1100 in JsonContext.java:41), so a 13-char literal like {\"a\":1E+2147483647} passes and produces a BigDecimal with scale near Integer.MIN_VALUE, reachable from an untrusted HTTP entity (JsonValueBodyReader.java:69-70 with Json.createReaderFactory(null), JsonParserImpl.java:171). However the lead's stated DoS mechanism does not survive scrutiny for its primary sinks: BigDecimal.intValueExact()/longValueExact() must throw ArithmeticException for any value that does not fit a long and determine that from precision()/scale() arithmetic before any setScale(0) (precision()-scale > 19 for moderate exponents; and for |scale| ~ 2^31 that subtraction overflows int to a negative value, hitting the fraction-only/\"Rounding necessary\" throw) - so no ~2^31-digit unscaled value is ever materialized there; doubleValue() likewise only needs the exponent (parseDouble of the ~13-char scientific-notation toString) and returns Infinity cheaply. That leaves only intValue()/longValue() (lines 264, 274) as a plausible DoS, via a naive toBigInteger() -> bigTenToThe(|scale|) allocation, and whether the running JDK's BigDecimal.longValue() short-circuits large negative scales is exactly what I could not establish in this session: there is no JVM installed (/usr/lib/jvm does not exist), no JDK source on disk, and no execution tool available, so I could not run the PoC or read java.math.BigDecimal. Exploitation additionally requires application code to call intValue()/longValue() on the parsed number - parsson itself never does so while parsing (in-repo callers are only public API surfaces JsonTokenizer.java:544/559, JsonStructureParser.java:73/81, JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:264). I therefore confirm the guard asymmetry as a defense-in-depth gap but cannot confirm the claimed exploitable CPU/memory exhaustion, and I reject the specific intValueExact/longValueExact/doubleValue mechanism. exploit_search was unavailable (corpus not indexed), so no version cross-check for parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33) was possible."
          },
