          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING max-string/token-length limit in the tokenizer. org.eclipse.parsson.JsonTokenizer.fillBuf() grows its character buffer by unconditional doubling (`char[] doubleBuf = Arrays.copyOf(buf, 2 * buf.length)`) whenever a single JSON token does not fit the current buffer (guard is only `storeLen == buf.length`). There is NO upper bound on the resulting array size and no corresponding configuration knob anywhere in the library: JsonContext's complete control surface is exactly bigIntegerScaleLimit(), bigDecimalLengthLimit(), depthLimit(), prettyPrinting(), rejectDuplicateKeys(), bufferPool() -- there is no maxStringLength/maxNameLength/maxDocumentSize. A single unterminated or very long string/name/number token in attacker-supplied JSON therefore forces repeated doubling until OutOfMemoryError (CWE-770 / CWE-789 uncontrolled resource allocation). Aggravating detail: line 479 recycles the OLD small buffer into the shared BufferPool while the newly doubled buffer replaces it, so oversized buffers can later pollute the pool. fillBuf is attacker-reachable (reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=8).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: JsonTokenizer.fillBuf() grows its token buffer by unconditional doubling with the sole guard `storeLen == buf.length` and no maximum-size comparison, and no max-string/name/token/document limit exists anywhere in the library. I hand-traced the loop to prove it is live rather than dead code: readString() sets storeBegin=readBegin (line 132); the first fillBuf() takes the left-shift branch (483-486) setting storeBegin=0; once storeEnd reaches buf.length the doubling branch (476-480) fires, and reader.read(buf, storeEnd, buf.length-storeEnd) at line 496 refills exactly the newly added half, so every subsequent fill doubles again (4096 -> 8192 -> 16384 -> ...), bounded only by heap. At buf.length == 2^30, `2 * buf.length` overflows int and Arrays.copyOf receives a negative length (NegativeArraySizeException); otherwise OutOfMemoryError. Both termini are unchecked Error/RuntimeException, not a JsonParsingException, so a single oversized string/name/number token destabilizes the whole JVM instead of failing one request. No knob exists to bound it: JsonContext's accessors (112-134) are exactly bigIntegerScaleLimit, bigDecimalLengthLimit, depthLimit, prettyPrinting, rejectDuplicateKeys, bufferPool, and api/JsonConfig.java:19-52 defines only MAX_BIGINTEGER_SCALE, MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN, MAX_DEPTH, REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS; a repo-wide case-insensitive grep for maxString|stringLength|maxName|maxToken|maxDocument returned zero matches. The existing bigDecimalLengthLimit is enforced in getBigDecimal() at line 522, i.e. AFTER tokenization, so it does not prevent buffer growth, and the STRING path has no check at all (getValue(), 510-512). Reachability confirmed by my own Cypher query (fillBuf line 471, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=8) and by a concrete attacker-facing path shipped in this repo: rest/.../JsonValueBodyReader.java:69 is a JAX-RS @Provider consuming */* that feeds the raw HTTP request InputStream into rf.createReader(inputStream).readValue() with all-default config (line 45). Two corrections to the analyst: (a) the pool-pollution aggravator is overstated -- line 479 recycles the OLD buffer, which on the first doubling is the legitimately-pooled 4096 array, so pollution only arises from later doublings and from close() (585) recycling the final grown buffer, and BufferPoolImpl holds its queue via a WeakReference (line 32) so oversized arrays are GC-able; (b) severity is MEDIUM, not HIGH, because amplification is only linear (~4-8x wire bytes to heap bytes) -- the attacker must actually transmit the data, so this is not an amplification bomb. exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so I could not ground exploitability against published modules in either direction; version pinned as Parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33)."
          },
