          "ruleId": "CWE-344",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING nonce randomness in OAuth 1.0a request signing. org.eclipse.parsson.demos.twitter.TwitterObjectSearch builds its OAuth signature base-string parameter map with a hardcoded constant nonce: `put(\"oauth_nonce\", \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\")`, carrying an explicit `// TODO` comment in the source, alongside `put(\"oauth_signature_method\", \"HMAC-SHA1\")` and `put(\"oauth_timestamp\", \"\" + timestamp)`. OAuth 1.0a requires a per-request unique nonce; the anti-replay control is simply absent (no SecureRandom/UUID generation appears in the map initializer). Every signed request reuses the same nonce value, defeating server-side replay detection for the nonce+timestamp pair (CWE-323 reusing a nonce in a cryptographic protocol / CWE-330 insufficiently random values). Scope caveat for the verifier: this is demo/sample code under demos/twitter, not the shipped impl/ library, so real-world impact is limited to anyone copying the sample.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The described code structure is genuinely present and I re-derived every element independently. demos/twitter/.../TwitterObjectSearch.java:84 contains `put(\"oauth_nonce\", \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\"); // TODO` \u2014 a hardcoded invariant OAuth 1.0a nonce. A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for `oauth_nonce|SecureRandom|randomUUID|nonce` returns exactly ONE hit (that line), proving the randomness control is absent rather than implemented elsewhere. I traced the nonce into the signature: TreeMap (L81-90) -> param string (L93-104) -> HMAC-SHA1 base string (L107-120) -> Authorization header (L126-146), sent to https://api.twitter.com/1.1/search/tweets.json (L69). This violates RFC 5849 section 3.3, which requires the nonce be unique per request for a given timestamp/client/token tuple. LOCATION CORRECTED: the nonce put is at line 84, not the claimed 81; line 81 is the `new TreeMap` alloc, and the graph reports 81 only because Joern collapses the anonymous-subclass initializer onto the allocation site (that artifact is what misled the analyst). SEVERITY DOWNGRADED TO LOW, and CWE recast: (1) the nonce is client-generated anti-replay material consumed by Twitter's server \u2014 a constant value does not weaken the HMAC (key = consumerSecret&accessTokenSecret), leak credentials, or permit signature forgery; (2) it does not widen an attacker's replay window, since a captured request carries its own (nonce,timestamp) pair and server-side dedup catches exact duplicates either way \u2014 the real effect is that the client's OWN requests within the same one-second window collide and get rejected, a functional bug more than an attacker primitive; (3) scope is demo code, not the shipped impl/ library \u2014 a standalone CLI main() with a hardcoded \"#javaone\" query and no attacker-controlled input, whose twitterconfig.properties ships with empty credentials, against a Twitter v1.1 endpoint defunct since 2023. The graph's exposure:\"reachable\"/kind:\"handler\" on main() is scanner over-classification of a CLI entrypoint, not network exposure. CWE-323 presumes encryption where nonce+key reuse breaks confidentiality, which does not apply to an HMAC-signed protocol nonce; CWE-344 (use of invariant value in dynamically changing context) is the precise fit. Verdict: true-positive spec-conformance defect, not a practically exploitable vulnerability. Exploit-reference check could not be performed \u2014 the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no published-exploit evidence exists either for or against; component pins to org.eclipse.parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (root pom.xml:33), but this is demo-tree code rather than a library CVE question."
          },
