        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-636",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Json.createParserFactory(config) builds its JsonContext with a property whitelist of ONLY JsonContext.PROPERTY_BUFFER_POOL, omitting jakarta.json.JsonConfig.KEY_STRATEGY \u2014 while the sibling createReaderFactory (line 119) and createBuilderFactory (line 225) paths DO whitelist JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS and KEY_STRATEGY. Because JsonContext.config(String) reads from that whitelist-filtered map (`return config != null ? config.get(propertyName) : null`), a KEY_STRATEGY the caller supplied is silently discarded on the parser-factory path. JsonParserImpl.getObject()/getValue() builds objects via `new JsonObjectBuilderImpl(jsonContext)` (JsonParserImpl.java:152 and :161), and JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:53-54 resolves the duplicate-key policy as DuplicateStrategy.strategyFromProperty(jsonContext.config(KEY_STRATEGY), jsonContext.rejectDuplicateKeys()). With KEY_STRATEGY dropped to null and REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS unset, strategyFromProperty returns DuplicateStrategy.LAST, so duplicate object keys are silently accepted last-wins instead of being rejected. Net effect: the same config map that enforces duplicate-key rejection through Json.createReaderFactory(...) is a no-op through Json.createParserFactory(...) \u2014 a protection wired into two of three factory paths and omitted in the third, which is exactly the parser-disagreement primitive behind JSON duplicate-key auth-bypass / request-smuggling bugs.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The core defect is real and I verified every link in the chain against the source. JsonProviderImpl.createParserFactory (line 71) whitelists only JsonContext.PROPERTY_BUFFER_POOL, while the sibling createReaderFactory (119-122) and createBuilderFactory (225-228) both whitelist jakarta.json.JsonConfig.KEY_STRATEGY. JsonContext's varargs constructor stores copyPropertiesMap(this, config, properties) (JsonContext.java:100-101), copyPropertiesMap copies ONLY whitelisted names (219-245), and config(String) reads exclusively from that filtered map (108-110) -- so config(KEY_STRATEGY) is null on the parser-factory path. JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:53-54 resolves the duplicate-key policy from exactly that call, and strategyFromProperty (391-404) falls through to DuplicateStrategy.LAST when the value is null and rejectDuplicateKeys is false. The parser path reaches that constructor: JsonParserImpl.java:152 (getObject) and :161 (getValue) do `new JsonObjectBuilderImpl(jsonContext)`, and getObject(builder) at 320-331 calls builder.add -> putValueMap (197-203) -> duplicateStrategy.getValue. Net: Json.createParserFactory(Map.of(JsonConfig.KEY_STRATEGY, KeyStrategy.NONE)).createParser(r).getObject() silently applies last-wins instead of throwing IllegalStateException, whereas the identical map through createReaderFactory enforces it -- a security control wired into two of three factory paths and silently dropped in the third (CWE-636, not failing securely). Graph confirms reachability: all three JsonContext allocations reachable_from_entry=true, and putValueMap (hop 2) / strategyFromProperty (hop 4) reachable=true. No test exercises KEY_STRATEGY through createParserFactory (grep finds it only in JsonReaderTest.java:188,212,224,236). ONE CORRECTION to the analyst, which narrows the finding: the analyst claimed REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS is also \"unset\" and that the whole config map is a no-op on this path. That is false -- JsonContext.java:98 computes this.rejectDuplicateKeys from the RAW config argument before whitelisting, so the deprecated org.eclipse.parsson.api.JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS IS still honored through createParserFactory. Only the non-deprecated jakarta.json KEY_STRATEGY is discarded (both NONE -> no rejection, and FIRST -> becomes last-wins). Severity is MEDIUM rather than high: the bug is not attacker-reachable on its own -- it requires the application to opt into KEY_STRATEGY on the streaming JsonParser factory path and to call getObject()/getValue(); exploitation then depends on the app's own duplicate-key trust assumptions. exploit_search could not be used as corroboration (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so this verdict rests entirely on the source and graph evidence I gathered."
          },
