        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-484",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JsonContext.copyPropertiesMap contains a switch whose `case JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS:` branch is MISSING a `break;` \u2014 it conditionally puts the hardened value (`if (instance.rejectDuplicateKeys) newConfig.put(JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS, true)` at line 235) and then falls straight through into `default:` at line 240 (`if (config.containsKey(propertyName)) newConfig.put(propertyName, config.get(propertyName))`), which re-puts the SAME key with the caller's raw value and overwrites the just-written `true`. The immediately preceding `case JsonGenerator.PRETTY_PRINTING:` branch does have its `break;`, so the omission is an asymmetry, and the comment \"// Rest of properties are copied without changes\" sits exactly where the `break;` should be \u2014 i.e. the deliberate protection-normalization step is silently undone by the fall-through. Concretely, a caller passing Map.of(JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS, Boolean.FALSE) gets instance.rejectDuplicateKeys == true (because getBooleanProperty is presence-based: `config.containsKey(propertyName) ? true : null`) yet getConfigInUse() reports REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS=false, so the factory misreports its own duplicate-key protection state. Note for the verifier: the enforcement path JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:53 reads the `rejectDuplicateKeys` FIELD rather than this map, so the direct impact appears confined to getConfigInUse() misreporting rather than outright protection loss \u2014 rank accordingly.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source and confirmed. (1) The missing `break` is real: JsonContext.java:228-232 (`case JsonGenerator.PRETTY_PRINTING`) ends with `break;` at line 232, but `case JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS:` at 233 closes its if-block at line 236 and falls straight through the comment at 237 into `default:` at 238, where line 240 re-puts the SAME key with `config.get(propertyName)`, overwriting the normalized `true` written at line 235. (2) The case is NOT dead code -- a fact the analyst did not cite but which I verified: JsonProviderImpl.java:119-122 (createReaderFactory) and :225-228 (createBuilderFactory) both pass JsonConfig.REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS into the `properties` varargs of the JsonContext(config, pool, String...) constructor (JsonContext.java:93-102), so public API entry reaches this case. (3) Presence-based semantics confirmed at JsonContext.java:179-181 (`config.containsKey(propertyName) ? true : null`), so any non-null value sets the field `true` while the fall-through reports the caller's raw value -- divergence for any value other than `true`. (4) Crucially, the analyst's own caveat holds and I verified it exhaustively: a grep of all of impl/src/main/java shows the duplicate-key setting is read ONLY via the FIELD at JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:53-54 (`jsonContext.rejectDuplicateKeys()` -> strategyFromProperty, line 399); REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS is never read back out of the config map (the only map read is `config(jakarta.json.JsonConfig.KEY_STRATEGY)`). The repo's own test JsonReaderTest.java:198-208 proves enforcement is value-insensitive (value \"anything is valid here\" still throws JsonException on duplicates). So there is NO loss of duplicate-key protection and no trust boundary crossed; the impact is confined to getConfigInUse() (JsonReaderFactoryImpl.java:53-54, JsonBuilderFactoryImpl.java:70-71) returning a map that contradicts the factory's actual behavior -- a public Jakarta JSON-P API contract/correctness bug. I therefore CONFIRM the defect but assign LOW severity rather than treating it as a security control bypass. Version pinned at parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33); exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), immaterial since this is a first-party logic defect, not a versioned-dependency CVE."
