          "message": {
            "text": "Hardcoded Flickr API key embedded directly in a URL string literal: `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?method=flickr.photos.getRecent&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&format=json&nojsoncallback=1&per_page=20\")` in ParserResource.writeFlickerFeed. The credential `221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77` is a committed secret in source control, retrievable by anyone with read access to the repo or to the built artifact (string constants survive compilation into the class file). The enclosing method is reachable from the JAX-RS entry point ParserResource.doGet.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. (1) The hardcoded credential is present exactly as claimed: ParserResource.java:53 embeds a 32-hex Flickr API key `221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77` directly in a `new URL(...)` string literal; a tree-wide grep for the raw key string returns exactly this one occurrence, so the claimed location is accurate. (2) Reachability verified in the real source, not merely inferred from the graph: the class is `@Path(\"/parser\")` with `doGet()` annotated `@GET` (lines 39-44); `doGet` returns an anonymous `StreamingOutput` whose `write()` invokes `writeFlickerFeed(os)` at line 47, which executes line 53. The graph independently agrees - both `doGet` and the anonymous `StreamingOutput$0.write` are :EntryPoint nodes with exposure=\"reachable\", kind=\"handler\", and the line-53 call has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=3. (3) It is a real committed secret rather than a placeholder stub: the sibling demos externalize credentials properly (demos/facebook/.../facebookconfig.properties:12 `access_token=` empty, demos/twitter/.../twitterconfig.properties:12-13 `consumer-key=`/`consumer-secret=` empty, both read at runtime via config.get(...)), while demos/rest ships no properties file at all - so the inline key deviates from the project's own convention and is a well-formed Flickr key, not `YOUR_API_KEY_HERE`. I DOWNGRADE the