          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The same URL literal at ParserResource.java:53 uses the cleartext `http://` scheme (not `https://`) while carrying the `api_key` credential in the query string. The API key is therefore transmitted unencrypted over the network on every call to writeFlickerFeed, exposing it to any network observer or MITM, and it is additionally logged in plaintext by any intermediary proxy that records request URLs. This is a distinct defect from the hardcoding itself -- switching to https would not de-hardcode the key, and rotating the key would not fix the cleartext channel. Note this is the ONLY `http://` URL actually opened in the whole scan.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived from source: ParserResource.java:53 builds a URL with the cleartext `http://` scheme whose query string carries the `api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77` credential, and line 54 issues the request via `url.openStream()`, so the credential is transmitted unencrypted in the HTTP request line (and would be recorded by any intermediary proxy logging request URLs). This is genuinely distinct from the hardcoding defect: switching to https would not de-hardcode the key, and rotating the key would not encrypt the channel. Reachability confirmed in BOTH source and graph: `@Path(\"/parser\")` + `@GET doGet()` (L39-44) returns an anonymous StreamingOutput whose `write()` calls `writeFlickerFeed(os)` (L45-49), and the graph has `EntryPoint {kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\"}` -> `ParserResource.doGet` plus `...doGet.StreamingOutput$0.write`, with the L53 `new URL(...)` and L54 `openStream` calls each `reachable_from_entry:true, hop_distance:3`. The analyst's uniqueness claim also holds: repo-wide grep for `api_key|api.flickr.com` yields exactly one hit, and the graph query for `http://` in URL/openStream/assignment calls returns only this one site. I corrected two things: the line range should be 53-54 (the actual cleartext transmission happens at the `openStream()` on L54, not at the literal alone), and severity is LOW rather than HIGH -- this is sample/demo WAR code under `demos/rest` with its own standalone pom (the root pom builds the parsson library and does not aggregate demos, so it ships in no released artifact), the key is already public by being committed to an open-source repo, and the retrieved data is public Flickr photo metadata. The other `http://` strings in the file (L6 license header, L74 `<img src=\"http://farm...\">`) carry no credential and are not opened by this process, so they are not part of this finding. No versioned third-party component is named by this lead, so an exploit-catalog version comparison is not applicable."
