          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Inconsistent application of the bigIntegerScaleLimit guard across JsonNumberImpl accessors. The scale check `Math.abs(bd.scale()) <= bigIntegerScaleLimit` is applied ONLY in bigIntegerValue() (lines 290-291) and bigIntegerValueExact() (lines 300-301). The sibling accessors on the same class that also materialize the BigDecimal are NOT guarded: isIntegral() calls `bigDecimalValue().scale()` at line 259 and intValueExact() calls `bigDecimalValue().intValueExact()` at line 269, and per the semantic-search body text intValue()/longValue()/longValueExact()/doubleValue() are likewise unguarded. If any of those JDK BigDecimal accessors internally expands a hostile extreme-scale value (e.g. a parsed `1E-2147483647`) the scale cap is bypassed on that path. IMPORTANT CAVEAT for the verifier: whether this is actually exploitable depends on JDK BigDecimal internals (several of these have fast-paths that return 0 without expansion) AND on whether bigDecimalLengthLimit (default 1100) already rejects such a literal at parse time in JsonTokenizer -- I could not resolve either from the graph alone. Treat as a guard-asymmetry observation to confirm, not an established DoS.\n\nVerifier (INCONCLUSIVE): The guard asymmetry is real but is documented as intentional, and the only live hazard candidate cannot be settled in this session. (1) CONFIRMED in source: the `Math.abs(bd.scale()) <= bigIntegerScaleLimit` check exists only in bigIntegerValue() (JsonNumberImpl.java:287-295) and bigIntegerValueExact() (297-305); isIntegral/intValue/intValueExact/longValue/longValueExact/doubleValue (257-285) call bigDecimalValue().xxx() with no check. (2) But JsonConfig.java:21-28 explicitly documents the property as applying only to bigIntegerValue()/bigIntegerValueExact(), so the asymmetry is deliberate scope, not an oversight -- it is a defect only if an unguarded accessor actually expands. (3) The analyst's primary claimed location is DISPROVED: line 259 is isIntegral() -> BigDecimal.scale(), a plain field read with no materialization. intValue()/longValue()/doubleValue() likewise have well-known JDK zero fast-paths (fractionOnly(), scale <= -64, scientific-notation toString), and longValueExact()'s `(precision() - scale) > 19` check cheaply rejects the large-negative-scale case. (4) I DID resolve the analyst's parse-time caveat against them: JsonTokenizer.getBigDecimal() (519-531) limits only the number's source character count (default 1100, JsonContext.java:41), so `1E-100000000` (12 chars) parses fine to scale=100000000, JsonParserImpl.java:171 wraps it in JsonBigDecimalNumber which does not override intValueExact/longValueExact, and the graph shows both base-class methods reachable_from_entry=true. So a hostile extreme-positive-scale value does reach the unguarded *Exact accessors. (5) UNRESOLVED: whether JDK BigDecimal.longValueExact() short-circuits fraction-only values before setScale(0, ROUND_UNNECESSARY) -> bigTenToThe(scale) -> BigInteger.TEN.pow(1e8). That is purely JDK-internal; no java runtime, no Bash tool, and no JDK src.zip/BigDecimal.java exist on this machine, so I could neither read nor execute it. Circumstantially, the CVE-2023-4043 reporters audited this exact class and the maintainers still scoped the guard to only the two BigInteger accessors, which argues the rest are safe. Not enough self-gathered evidence to CONFIRM a DoS, and not enough to fully REJECT the intValueExact/longValueExact path. exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no version grounding was possible for Parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33)."
